<section class="col-xs-12 col-sm-12 col-md-12 col-lg-9 col-xl-9" id="main">
<div class="html" id="tag">
<h3 class="tex-subsection" id="subsection:compactly-generated"><span data-tag="0673">9.4.1</span> Compactly Generated $\infty $-Categories</h3>
<p>
In this section, we study $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which contain “enough” compact objects. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="0674">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0674">Definition <span data-tag="0674">9.4.1.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. We say that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>compactly generated</em> if it satisfies the following conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits small filtered colimits. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be realized as the colimit of a small filtered diagram $\{  C_{\alpha } \} $, where each $C_{\alpha }$ is a compact object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="06GV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06GV">Example <span data-tag="06GV">9.4.1.2</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ denote the $\infty $-category of spaces (Construction <a data-tag="00TZ" href="/tag/00TZ">3.1.6.1</a>). Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ is compactly generated. It admits small colimits (Corollary <a data-tag="02VF" href="/tag/02VF">7.4.3.3</a> and every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ can be realized as a small filtered colimit of essentially finite Kan complexes (Proposition <a data-tag="06FT" href="/tag/06FT">9.3.2.20</a>), which are compact by virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="067U" href="/tag/067U">9.2.6.5</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="06GW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06GW">Example <span data-tag="06GW">9.4.1.3</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ denote the $\infty $-category of (small) $\infty $-categories (Construction <a data-tag="0209" href="/tag/0209">5.5.3.1</a>). Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ is compactly generated. It admits small colimits (Corollary <a data-tag="02V1" href="/tag/02V1">7.4.5.3</a>), and every object $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\in \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ can be realized as a small filtered colimits of essentially finite $\infty $-categories (Proposition <a data-tag="06FV" href="/tag/06FV">9.3.2.24</a>), which are compact by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="068G" href="/tag/068G">9.2.7.9</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="0675">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0675">Example <span data-tag="0675">9.4.1.4</span>.</a> Every Kan complex $X$ is compactly generated when viewed as an $\infty $-category: see Example <a data-tag="064E" href="/tag/064E">9.2.5.5</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="02XX" href="/tag/02XX">7.2.3.5</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="0676">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0676">Definition <span data-tag="0676">9.4.1.5</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be a small regular cardinal and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. We say that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>$\kappa $-compactly generated</em> if it satisfies the following conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a_{\kappa })$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits small $\kappa $-filtered colimits. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b_{\kappa })$</dt>
<dd><p>
Every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be realized as the colimit of a small $\kappa $-filtered diagram $\{  C_{\alpha } \} $, where each $C_{\alpha }$ is a $\kappa $-compact object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="0677">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0677">Example <span data-tag="0677">9.4.1.6</span>.</a> An $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compactly generated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0674" href="/tag/0674">9.4.1.1</a>) if and only if it is $\aleph _0$-compactly generated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0676" href="/tag/0676">9.4.1.5</a>). See Example <a data-tag="064H" href="/tag/064H">9.2.5.8</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-variant" id="0678">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0678">Variant <span data-tag="0678">9.4.1.7</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. We say that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is <em>$(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated</em> if it satisfies the following conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a_{\kappa ,\lambda })$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-cocomplete: that is, it admits $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered colimits. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b_{\kappa ,\lambda })$</dt>
<dd><p>
Every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be realized as the colimit of a $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered diagram $\{  C_{\alpha } \} $, where each $C_{\alpha }$ is a $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="0679">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0679">Remark <span data-tag="0679">9.4.1.8</span>.</a> Following the convention of Remark <a data-tag="03PU" href="/tag/03PU">4.9.0.4</a>, a regular cardinal $\kappa $ is small if it satisfies $\kappa &lt;  \Omega $, for some fixed strongly inaccessible cardinal $ \Omega $. In this case, an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\kappa $-compactly generated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0676" href="/tag/0676">9.4.1.5</a>) if and only if it is $(\kappa , \Omega )$-compactly generated (in the sense of Variant <a data-tag="0678" href="/tag/0678">9.4.1.7</a>). In particular, $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compactly generated if and only if it is $(\aleph _0,  \Omega )$-compactly generated. See Remark <a data-tag="064P" href="/tag/064P">9.2.5.14</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="067A">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067A">Example <span data-tag="067A">9.4.1.9</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be an uncountable regular cardinal. An $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\kappa )$-compactly generated if and only if it is idempotent-complete (see Example <a data-tag="064Q" href="/tag/064Q">9.2.5.15</a>). If $\kappa = \aleph _0$, every $\infty $-category is $(\kappa ,\kappa )$-compactly generated. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06GX">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06GX">Remark <span data-tag="06GX">9.4.1.10</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda $ be regular cardinals and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated if and only if it is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-cocomplete and satisfies the following stronger version of condition $(b_{\kappa ,\lambda })$: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(b_{\kappa ,\lambda }^{+} )$</dt>
<dd><p>
Every object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be realized as the colimit of a diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(A) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\quad \quad (\alpha \in A) \mapsto \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{\alpha }, \]
</div>
<p>
 where $(A,\leq )$ is a $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-directed partially ordered set and each $C_{\alpha }$ is a $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> This follows from Theorem <a data-tag="02QA" href="/tag/02QA">9.1.8.7</a> (for the case $\kappa \triangleleft \lambda $) and Example <a data-tag="067A" href="/tag/067A">9.4.1.9</a> (for the case $\kappa = \lambda $). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="067B">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067B">Proposition <span data-tag="067B">9.4.1.11</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a functor of $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}' \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}'$ (Variant <a data-tag="063R" href="/tag/063R">9.3.1.7</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ for which the inclusion functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Moreover, if these conditions are satisfied, then we can take $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ to be the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ spanned by the $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact objects. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implication $(3) \Rightarrow (2)$ is immediate, and the implication $(2) \Rightarrow (1)$ follows from Proposition <a data-tag="0653" href="/tag/0653">9.3.2.3</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="0666" href="/tag/0666">9.3.4.26</a>. To complete the proof, assume that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the full subcategory spanned by the $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact objects. Applying the criterion of Proposition <a data-tag="0653" href="/tag/0653">9.3.2.3</a>, we conclude that the inclusion functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="06GY">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06GY">Example <span data-tag="06GY">9.4.1.12</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda $ be regular cardinals, where $\lambda $ is uncountable. Then the $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \lambda }$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \lambda }$ are $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated. This follows by combining Proposition <a data-tag="067B" href="/tag/067B">9.4.1.11</a> with Variants <a data-tag="06FU" href="/tag/06FU">9.3.2.21</a> and <a data-tag="06FW" href="/tag/06FW">9.3.2.25</a>, respectively (together with Remark <a data-tag="068R" href="/tag/068R">9.3.2.23</a> in the special case $\kappa = \aleph _0$). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="067C">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067C">Corollary <span data-tag="067C">9.4.1.13</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be a small regular cardinal and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\kappa $-compactly generated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a functor of $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}' \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}'$ (Definition <a data-tag="063N" href="/tag/063N">9.3.1.4</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ for which the inclusion functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Moreover, if these conditions are satisfied, then we can take $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 = \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ to be the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ spanned by the $\kappa $-compact objects. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Proposition <a data-tag="067B" href="/tag/067B">9.4.1.11</a> in the special case where $\lambda =  \Omega $ is a strongly inaccessible cardinal (Remark <a data-tag="0679" href="/tag/0679">9.4.1.8</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="067D">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067D">Corollary <span data-tag="067D">9.4.1.14</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compactly generated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a functor of $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}' \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}'$ (Definition <a data-tag="063K" href="/tag/063K">9.3.1.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists a full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ for which the inclusion functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Moreover, if these conditions are satisfied, then we can take $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 = \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{&lt; \aleph _0}$ to be the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ spanned by the compact objects. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Corollary <a data-tag="067C" href="/tag/067C">9.4.1.13</a> in the special case $\kappa = \aleph _0$ (see Example <a data-tag="0677" href="/tag/0677">9.4.1.6</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="067E">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067E">Remark <span data-tag="067E">9.4.1.15</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-cocomplete. To show that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated, it suffices to verify the following (which is <em>a priori</em> weaker than condition $(b_{\kappa ,\lambda })$ of Variant <a data-tag="0678" href="/tag/0678">9.4.1.7</a>): </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(b_{\kappa ,\lambda }^{-})$</dt>
<dd><p>
Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the full subcategory spanned by the $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact objects. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ generated $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ under $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered colimits. That is, if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}' \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a replete full subcategory which contains $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ and is closed under $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered colimits, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}' = \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> In this case, the recognition principle of Proposition <a data-tag="0653" href="/tag/0653">9.3.2.3</a> guarantees that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }$-completion of the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="06GZ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06GZ">Remark <span data-tag="06GZ">9.4.1.16</span> <span class="named">(Colimits of Compact Objects)</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{0} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be the full subcategory spanned by the $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact objects. Then the inclusion functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ creates $\kappa $-small colimits (Definition <a data-tag="02K8" href="/tag/02K8">7.1.4.23</a>). In other words, a $\kappa $-small diagram $K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ admits a colimit in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ if and only if it admits a colimit in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, in which case the colimits are the same (see Proposition <a data-tag="064W" href="/tag/064W">9.2.5.24</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="06N9" href="/tag/06N9">9.3.5.27</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="06H0">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06H0">Corollary <span data-tag="06H0">9.4.1.17</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be an $\infty $-category which is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-cocomplete. Then a functor $G: \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-finitary if and only if it satisfies the following condition: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(\ast )$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact object $C \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the composition 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\xrightarrow {G} \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\xrightarrow { \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( C, \bullet ) } \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \mu }  \]
</div>
<p>
 is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-finitary. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> Here $\mu \geq \lambda $ denotes any regular cardinal for which $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $\mu $-small. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{&lt; \kappa } \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ spanned by the $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact objects. It follows from Propositions <a data-tag="067B" href="/tag/067B">9.4.1.11</a> and <a data-tag="04BQ" href="/tag/04BQ">8.4.6.8</a>, that the restricted Yoneda embedding 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ h_{\bullet }: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}_{&lt; \kappa }, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \mu } ) \quad \quad X \mapsto \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(\bullet , X) \]
</div>
<p>
       is an equivalence from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to the smallest full subcategory of $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}_{&lt; \kappa }, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \mu } )$ which contains all representable functors and is closed under $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered colimits. It follows that $G$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-finitary if and only if the composition $h_{\bullet } \circ G$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-finitary. The equivalence of this condition with $(\ast )$ follows from the observation that $\mu $-small colimits in $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \mu } )$ are computed levelwise (Proposition <a data-tag="02X9" href="/tag/02X9">7.1.8.3</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
<p>
Beware that, in the setting of Proposition <a data-tag="067B" href="/tag/067B">9.4.1.11</a>, the subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is generally not unique. However, it is unique up to Morita equivalence: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="067K">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067K">Proposition <span data-tag="067K">9.4.1.18</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals, where $\lambda $ is uncountable. Then the construction $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\mapsto \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ induces a bijection </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \{  \textnormal{Idempotent-complete $\infty $-categories} \}  / \textnormal{Equivalence} \ar [d]^{\sim } \\ \{  \textnormal{$(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated $\infty $-categories} \}  / \textnormal{Equivalence}. } \]
</div>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Surjectivity follows from Proposition <a data-tag="067B" href="/tag/067B">9.4.1.11</a>, and injectivity from Proposition <a data-tag="067G" href="/tag/067G">9.3.2.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="067L">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067L">Corollary <span data-tag="067L">9.4.1.19</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be a small regular cardinal. Then the construction $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\mapsto \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ induces a bijection </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \{  \textnormal{Idempotent-complete $\infty $-categories} \}  / \textnormal{Equivalence} \ar [d]^{\sim } \\ \{  \textnormal{$\kappa $-compactly generated $\infty $-categories} \}  / \textnormal{Equivalence}. } \]
</div>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Proposition <a data-tag="067K" href="/tag/067K">9.4.1.18</a> in the special case where $\lambda =  \Omega $ is a strongly inaccessible cardinal (Remark <a data-tag="0679" href="/tag/0679">9.4.1.8</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="067M">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067M">Corollary <span data-tag="067M">9.4.1.20</span>.</a> The construction $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\mapsto \operatorname{Ind}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ induces a bijection </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \{  \textnormal{Idempotent-complete $\infty $-categories} \}  / \textnormal{Equivalence} \ar [d]^{\sim } \\ \{  \textnormal{Compactly generated $\infty $-categories} \}  / \textnormal{Equivalence}. } \]
</div>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Corollary <a data-tag="067L" href="/tag/067L">9.4.1.19</a> in the special case $\kappa = \aleph _0$ (Example <a data-tag="0677" href="/tag/0677">9.4.1.6</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="06H1">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06H1">Proposition <span data-tag="06H1">9.4.1.21</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda \trianglelefteq \mu $ be regular cardinals and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. If the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated, then the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Ind}_{\lambda }^{\mu }( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is $(\kappa ,\mu )$-compactly generated. The converse holds if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is idempotent-complete. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated, then it can be identified with $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0)$ for some $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ (Proposition <a data-tag="067B" href="/tag/067B">9.4.1.11</a>). Applying Theorem <a data-tag="068Y" href="/tag/068Y">9.3.6.4</a>, we conclude that $\operatorname{Ind}_{\lambda }^{\mu }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is equivalent to $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\mu }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0)$, and is therefore $(\kappa ,\mu )$-compactly generated. 
    <p>
      We now prove the converse. Assume that $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} = \operatorname{Ind}_{\lambda }^{\mu }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is $(\kappa ,\mu )$-compactly generated: that is, we have an equivalence $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \simeq \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\mu }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0)$ for some other $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$. If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is idempotent-complete, then it can be identified with the full subcategory of $\widehat{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ spanned by the $(\lambda ,\mu )$-compact objects (Corollary <a data-tag="067J" href="/tag/067J">9.3.2.8</a>). Using Corollary <a data-tag="0691" href="/tag/0691">9.3.6.7</a>, we can identify this $\infty $-category with $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0)$, so that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="06H2">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06H2">Corollary <span data-tag="06H2">9.4.1.22</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda \trianglelefteq \mu $ be regular cardinals and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\mu )$-compactly generated if and only if it satisfies the following pair of conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\lambda ,\mu )$-compactly generated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{&lt; \lambda } \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ spanned by the $(\lambda ,\mu )$-compact objects is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Suppose first that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\mu )$-compactly generated: that is, it can be identified with the $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\mu }$-completion of some $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. Applying Theorem <a data-tag="068Y" href="/tag/068Y">9.3.6.4</a>, we conclude that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can also be identified with the $\operatorname{Ind}_{\lambda }^{\mu }$-completion of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$, and is therefore also $(\lambda ,\mu )$-compactly generated. 
    <p>
      Conversely, suppose that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\lambda ,\mu )$-compactly generated: that is, it can be identified with the $\operatorname{Ind}_{\lambda }^{\mu }$-completion of the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{&lt; \lambda } \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. In this case, we wish to show that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\mu )$-compactly generated if and only if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{&lt; \lambda }$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compactly generated. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $\mu $ is uncountable (otherwise, both conditions are vacuous). In this case, the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is idempotent-complete. Since the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{&lt; \lambda }$ is closed under retracts (Remark <a data-tag="064Z" href="/tag/064Z">9.2.5.18</a>), it is also also idempotent-complete (Proposition <a data-tag="040B" href="/tag/040B">8.5.4.10</a>). The desired equivalence now follows by applying Proposition <a data-tag="06H1" href="/tag/06H1">9.4.1.21</a> to the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{&lt; \lambda }$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></article>
